# The whole sudo grant on this host, and it is two exact command lines.
#
# ⚠ THIS FILE REVERSES A DOCUMENTED DECISION. roles/claude-code/tasks/main.yml says the
# session user "deliberately gets NO sudo — a session that can reconfigure the host it runs
# on can also undo the boundary it was given." That still holds for everything except these
# two lines, and it is why the grant is written the way it is:
#
#   · NO wildcards. sudoers matches the FULL command line, so `next-item` and `mint-token`
#     are spelled out and nothing else is reachable. A subcommand taking a flag would need
#     a `*` here, and a `*` in sudoers is how a narrow grant becomes a wide one.
#   · NO `ALL`, no command directories, no `!` negations. A negated blacklist under a
#     wildcard is bypassable; an explicit two-entry whitelist is not.
#   · NOPASSWD because {{ claude_user }} has no password to type — the account exists to
#     run units and accept key-based SSH.
#
# What this grant buys a session in bypassPermissions: a one-hour GitHub installation token
# and the text of the labelled work items. What it does not: the App private key, the Plane
# PAT, or any other command as root. Branch protection is what caps the token — see
# docs/runbooks/claude-loop.md.
#
# Validated with `visudo -cf` before it is installed. A syntax error in a sudoers file can
# lock the host out of sudo entirely, so the task never copies one it has not parsed.
{{ claude_user }} ALL=(root) NOPASSWD: {{ claude_loop_broker }} next-item, {{ claude_loop_broker }} mint-token
